\section{Parameters}{

Each of the wrapped family's distributional parameters is copied once
per state and suffixed with the state index, exactly as \code{\link[frmtmb:mixture]{frmtmb::mixture()}}
does: \code{hmm(2, gaussian())} has \code{mu1}, \code{mu2}, \code{sigma1}, \code{sigma2}. The
main model formula applies to every state's location parameter;
override one state with \code{bf(y ~ x, mu2 ~ x + (1 | id))}. Every dpar
takes the full formula grammar, random effects included.

Transition probabilities are a row-wise multinomial logit with
\strong{state 1 as the reference cell in every row}, named \verb{tr\{i\}\{j\}} for
the move from state \code{i} to state \code{j} (\code{j >= 2}): a two-state chain has
\code{tr12} and \code{tr22}. This is \code{depmixS4}'s parameterization, and the
coefficients agree with it to four decimals (\code{dev/hmm-feasibility.md},
probe B1). \code{hmmTMB} and \code{moveHMM} instead reference the diagonal;
the two span the same model and only the reported coefficients differ.
\code{trans} gives every transition cell the same default formula; a single
cell is overridden the ordinary way, \code{bf(y ~ 1, tr12 ~ x)}. The
covariate value at time \code{t} drives the transition from \code{t} to
\code{t + 1}, so a sequence's last row never contributes a transition -
again \code{depmixS4}'s convention.
