\section{Decoding and the fitted values}{

\code{E[y_t]} under an HMM is \verb{sum_k P(S_t = k | y) mu_k(x_t)}, which needs
the smoothed state probability and therefore a backward pass over the
whole sequence. \code{\link[=hmm_probs]{hmm_probs()}} returns those probabilities and
\code{\link[=hmm_viterbi]{hmm_viterbi()}} the maximum-a-posteriori state path; \code{\link[=fitted]{fitted()}},
\code{predict(type = "response")} and \code{\link[=residuals]{residuals()}} all route through
\code{\link[=hmm_probs]{hmm_probs()}}, so they report the occupancy-weighted mean rather than
any single state's.
}

\section{Label switching and local optima}{

The likelihood is invariant to permuting the states, so the state
means start at spread response quantiles (see \code{\link[frmtmb:mixture]{frmtmb::mixture()}}); a start
with every state mean equal sits on the symmetry axis and the
optimizer never leaves it. Relabeling between runs is expected and is
not fought.

Multimodality is real and NO convergence diagnostic reports it. On a
two-state model with a random intercept in each state mean, 25
sequences of 30, the default cold start converges to
-1096.09575602 where the optimum is -1087.99646521: \strong{8.099
log-likelihood units below it}, with \code{convergence == 0}, a positive
definite Hessian, no non-finite standard error, a largest gradient
of 7.00028e-04 (6.4e-07 of the log-likelihood), and \code{diagnose()}
printing "No convergence problems detected". That last clause is
true of this fit by a factor of 1.43: \code{diagnose()}'s clean verdict
needs \verb{max|grad|} under 1e-3 ABSOLUTE, and a construction whose
gradient landed just the other side of that would get a warning
instead. What is 8.099 units wrong here is the answer, not the
gradient, and no threshold on a gradient can see that. The optimum is confirmed
independently by a hand-rolled \code{MakeADFun(random =)} and by hmmTMB
to the last digit (\code{dev/hmm-feasibility.md}, probe D4;
\verb{dev/latent-2p3-repro81.R} reproduces it against this family).

\code{\link[=hmm_starts]{hmm_starts()}} is the remedy: it refits from jittered starting
values, returns the best, and reports the spread of the OPTIMA the
refits reached. On that probe, \code{hmm_starts(fit, n = 8, jitter = 2)}
recovered the better optimum on 5 of 5 seeds, and so did \code{jitter = 1}, \code{4} and \code{8}; \code{jitter = 0.5} recovered it on 1 of 5, which is
what the default of 2 is chosen from.

\code{\link[frmtmb:frm_allfit]{frm_allfit()}} is a different check, and on this probe it is a
reassuring one: it re-runs every optimizer from the SAME start, so it
tests the optimizer rather than the surface. All four (nlminb, optim,
bobyqa, NLopt L-BFGS) reach -1096.096 with \code{convergence == 0} and a
log-likelihood spread of 7.8e-07, which is agreement on the wrong
answer (\verb{dev/latent-2p3-allfit.R}).
}

